  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 255 AM MST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nA more active weather pattern is in store for the Tri-State \narea next week. Synoptically, we begin with a ridge centered \nover the upper Mississippi Valley on Tuesday. This system looks \nto be positively tilted as a longwave trough moves across the \nwestern CONUS and pushes said ridge eastward. Southwest flow \naloft should result as our county warning area (CWA) sits right \nin between the two Rossby waves. On Wednesday, the \naforementioned trough deamplifies quite a bit, sending a fairly \nstrong upper-level jet through our region. Guidance is a little \nmore uncertain after this process but most ensembles have weak \nridging that forms Thursday before another trough moves onshore \nover the Pacific Northwest. This next troughing pattern brings \nwith it another closed low aloft late next week that will define\nconditions for our area, dependent on the track and strength of\nthe system overall. Models are still in disagreement on these \ntwo parameters at the moment.\n\nThere will be two opportunities for rain during this four-day \nperiod. The first of which presents via a surface low that is \nforecasted to move north of the CWA on Wednesday with trough #1.\nThere should be a cold front with this system but guidance \ncurrently has dew points in the 30s after it passes so any \nprecipitation would be pre-frontal. At the moment, there is low \nconfidence of rain with this first system as Probability of \nPrecipitation (PoPs) stand at around 10-20%. Trough #2 should be\naccompanied by another low- pressure system on Friday that \ncould bring another front through the Tri-State area. Confidence\non rainfall is surprisingly high given the uncertain track of \nthe low. PoPs are currently 50-70% throughout the entire CWA but\ntrends regarding evolution of this system will need to be \nmonitored heading into next week.\n\nAnother potential result from this upbeat synoptic pattern is \nboosted surface winds. Two different upper-level jets are \nforecasted to influence our area, ushering in breezy conditions \nat the surface. As the first jet passes overhead Tuesday, 20-35 \nmph southerly winds are likely. Gusts up to 40 mph will be \npossible too, especially in eastern parts of the CWA. \nWednesday's winds should be just as strong, but with the \nstronger gusts located in the Colorado counties as well as east \nof US-83. There is a low chance(20%) of gusts up to 45 mph on \nWednesday. Winds die down Thursday after the first system \ndeparts but 10-20 mph is still in the realm of possibilities. \nFriday's system should bring more windy conditions with a \ngeneral 20- 30 anticipated. Gusts in the 45-50 mph will be \npossible in eastern Colorado.\n\nTemperatures are likely to be average or even slightly above \naverage for this time of the year. Highs in the mid to upper 50s\nTuesday should warm into 60s Wednesday. Thursdays high temps \nsit in the 52- 60 degree range and Friday shows consistent 60s \nas well. Nighttime lows will start warmer on Wednesday in the \n35-42 range but will fall quite a bit after the first cold front\nThursday morning with 30s expected across the board. Some \nlocations in the western CWA may drop into the 20s and other \nlocations east of Colby may only fall into the upper 30s. \nNevertheless, Friday morning low should bounce back a bit into \nthe upper 30s/low 40s. Fire weather should remain a non-factor \nfor the time being with RHs generally above 40%. Fog may be \npossible with RHs reaching 100% Wednesday and Friday mornings.",
